---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import AchievementsSection from '../components/AchievementsSection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Introduction"
  description="Tobby Smith - Software Development Leader with 24+ years of experience building high-performance web applications at scale and leading engineering teams."
  currentPage="home"
>
  <Hero
    name="Tobby Smith"
    title="Software Development Leader"
    tagline="Engineering leader with 24+ years of experience building high-performance web applications at scale and leading teams that deliver exceptional results."
    availability="Open to Director of Engineering & Software Development Manager roles"
  />

  <!-- About Me -->
  <section class="relative py-16 md:py-24 bg-surface-900 overflow-hidden" aria-labelledby="summary-heading">
    <div class="absolute inset-0" aria-hidden="true">
      <img
        src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1497366216548-37526070297c?w=1920&q=80&auto=format"
        alt=""
        class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-[0.25]"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-surface-900 via-surface-900/40 to-surface-900/60"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="relative max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
      <h2 id="summary-heading" class="text-[1.75rem] md:text-[2.5rem] font-display font-bold text-text-primary text-center mb-8">
        About Me
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg md:text-xl text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
        I turn legacy platforms into modern engineering organizations. At Zappos, that meant migrating a decade-old frontend to Next.js while keeping the site running for millions of customers — and making my team faster, not just the code.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg md:text-xl text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mx-auto text-center">
        I believe in shipping and iterating. Plans are great, but code in production teaches you more than any design doc. My teams move fast, learn from real users, and improve continuously.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Case Study: The Zappos Migration -->
  <section class="relative py-16 md:py-24 bg-surface-blue overflow-hidden" aria-labelledby="case-study-heading">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-br from-accent-500/10 via-transparent to-transparent" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <p class="text-accent-400 font-medium text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-center mb-3">Case Study</p>
      <h2 id="case-study-heading" class="text-[2rem] md:text-[3rem] font-display font-black text-text-primary text-center mb-12">
        The Zappos Migration
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-6 text-lg md:text-xl text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          When I joined, Zappos.com ran on an aging custom framework that was slow for customers and painful for developers. Build times were measured in minutes. Feature work crawled. Onboarding a new engineer took two months before they could ship anything meaningful.
        </p>
        <p>
          I led the migration to Next.js — not as a big-bang rewrite, but incrementally, page by page, while the site continued serving millions of customers. We shipped continuously, validated with real traffic, and rolled back when something didn't work.
        </p>
        <p class="text-text-primary font-medium border-l-4 border-accent-500 pl-6 my-8">
          The result: pages load 20% faster, Largest Contentful Paint improved 40%, and my team delivers features 35% faster than before. But the metric I'm most proud of is onboarding — down from two months to under one week — because that compounds every time we hire.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <AchievementsSection />

  <!-- How I Lead -->
  <section class="relative py-16 md:py-24 bg-surface-indigo overflow-hidden" aria-labelledby="leadership-heading">
    <div class="absolute inset-0" aria-hidden="true">
      <img
        src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1522071820081-009f0129c71c?w=1920&q=80&auto=format"
        alt=""
        class="w-full h-full object-cover opacity-[0.25]"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-surface-indigo via-surface-indigo/40 to-surface-indigo/60"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <h2 id="leadership-heading" class="text-[1.75rem] md:text-[2.5rem] font-display font-bold text-text-primary text-center mb-12">
        How I Lead
      </h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-surface-700 bg-surface-800 p-8 transition-all duration-200 hover:border-accent-500/30">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold text-accent-400 mb-4">Ship and iterate</h3>
          <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            I'd rather ship something good on Tuesday than something perfect next quarter. Real users teach you what matters faster than any planning exercise. My teams deploy continuously and treat production as a feedback loop.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-surface-700 bg-surface-800 p-8 transition-all duration-200 hover:border-accent-500/30">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-display font-bold text-accent-400 mb-4">Give context, not tasks</h3>
          <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Engineers make better decisions when they understand the why. I give my team ownership of problems, not tickets. They know the customers, the constraints, and the tradeoffs — and they consistently find solutions I wouldn't have prescribed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
